<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Basilica of Saint-Denis</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Abbey of Saint-Denis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Basilica of Saint-Denis, north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, began as the church of a royal abbey built over the tomb of Saint Denis, the 3rd-century first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Paris. From the Merovingian period it enjoyed the patronage of the Frankish kings and became the burial place of the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Its rebuilding under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suger">Abbot Suger</a> in the 1130s and 1140s is considered the beginning of Gothic architecture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/basilica-of-saint-denis.jpg" alt="Basilica of Saint-Denis" width="320" height="485" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thomas Clouet · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint-Denis_-_Fa%C3%A7ade.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Louis VI the Fat (r. 1108–1137), advised by Abbot Suger of <b>Saint-Denis</b>, subdued the defiant castellans of the Île-de-France and made the domain safe to travel.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The church grew immensely rich through royal and aristocratic gifts, and monasteries multiplied, especially after the Irish monk Columbanus founded Luxeuil around 590; the abbey of <b>Saint-Denis</b> near Paris, where Dagobert I was buried in 639, became closely tied to the crown.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-franks">Kingdom of the Franks · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>When King Aistulf seized Ravenna in 751 and threatened Rome, Pope Stephen II crossed the Alps and in 754 met the Frankish king Pepin the Short, whom he anointed at <b>Saint-Denis</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…sacramentary written for canonesses at Essen, with the Greek words in Latin letters and early neumes, and the Royal <b>Abbey of Saint-Denis</b> sang the hymn in Greek and in Latin at Pentecost and in its patron's festal week, after Saint Denis had become identified with Pseudo-Dionysius the Areopagite.</blockquote><a href="/songs/cherubikon">Cherubikon (the Cherubic Hymn)</a></li><li><blockquote>Hugh the Great held counties and lay abbacies around Paris, Orléans and Tours, including Saint-Martin of Tours and <b>Saint-Denis</b>, giving him more land and men than the king.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-the-franks">Duchy of the Franks</a></li><li><blockquote>Neustria had a strongly Gallo-Roman population and rich abbeys such as <b>Saint-Denis</b>, and in the 7th century its mayor Ebroin dominated politics.</blockquote><a href="/state/neustria">Neustria</a></li></ul></section>
